# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Define the library's HTTP contract before writing its Spring implementation, then use OpenAPI
Generator and Java compilation as fast feedback on the design.

## Outcome

You will replace the template's placeholder Items API with create, retrieve, and bounded list
operations for books. The build will generate a `BooksApi` interface and API models from the
contract. No controller is added until Tutorial 3.

## Prerequisites

- Complete [Tutorial 1](java-tutorial-01-create-service.html)
- Amazon Corretto Java 25
- The companion service builds with `./gradlew clean build`

## Fast track

1. Replace the placeholder contract with the Books API contract shown below.
2. Remove the template's placeholder Item implementation and its tests; it no longer matches the
   public contract.
3. Run `./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api:openApiGenerate`.
4. Inspect `BooksApi`, `Book`, `CreateBookRequest`, `BookList`, and `ErrorResponse` under the API
   module's `generated/` directory without editing them.
5. Run the API module's `compileJava` task, complete the ISBN challenge, then run a clean build.

## Start with the contract

Replace `laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api/open-api-specification.yml` with this contract:

```yaml
openapi: "3.0.3"
info:
  title: Library Books API
  version: 1.0.0
paths:
  /api/v1/books:
    post:
      operationId: createBook
      tags: [books]
      requestBody:
        required: true
        content:
          application/json:
            schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/CreateBookRequest' }
      responses:
        '201':
          description: Book created
          content:
            application/json:
              schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/Book' }
        '400': { $ref: '#/components/responses/BadRequest' }
    get:
      operationId: listBooks
      tags: [books]
      parameters:
        - $ref: '#/components/parameters/Limit'
      responses:
        '200':
          description: A bounded list of books
          content:
            application/json:
              schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/BookList' }
  /api/v1/books/{bookId}:
    get:
      operationId: getBook
      tags: [books]
      parameters:
        - name: bookId
          in: path
          required: true
          schema: { type: integer, format: int64, minimum: 1 }
      responses:
        '200':
          description: Book found
          content:
            application/json:
              schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/Book' }
        '404': { $ref: '#/components/responses/NotFound' }
components:
  parameters:
    Limit:
      name: limit
      in: query
      schema: { type: integer, minimum: 1, maximum: 100, default: 20 }
  responses:
    BadRequest:
      description: Request validation failed
      content:
        application/json:
          schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/ErrorResponse' }
    NotFound:
      description: The requested resource was not found
      content:
        application/json:
          schema: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/ErrorResponse' }
  schemas:
    Book:
      type: object
      required: [id, title]
      properties:
        id: { type: integer, format: int64, minimum: 1, readOnly: true }
        title: { type: string, minLength: 1, maxLength: 200, pattern: '.*\S.*' }
        publicationYear: { type: integer, minimum: 1450, maximum: 2100 }
        isbn:
          type: string
          pattern: '^(?:[0-9]{9}[0-9X]|[0-9]{13})$'
          example: "9781473230982"
    CreateBookRequest:
      type: object
      required: [title]
      properties:
        title: { type: string, minLength: 1, maxLength: 200, pattern: '.*\S.*' }
        publicationYear: { type: integer, minimum: 1450, maximum: 2100 }
        isbn: { type: string, pattern: '^(?:[0-9]{9}[0-9X]|[0-9]{13})$' }
    BookList:
      type: object
      required: [books, limit]
      properties:
        books:
          type: array
          maxItems: 100
          items: { $ref: '#/components/schemas/Book' }
        limit: { type: integer, minimum: 1, maximum: 100 }
    ErrorResponse:
      type: object
      required: [status, code, message]
      properties:
        status: { type: integer, format: int32, minimum: 400, maximum: 599 }
        code: { type: string, maxLength: 100 }
        message: { type: string, maxLength: 500 }
```

It defines these operations:

| Method and path | Operation ID | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `POST /api/v1/books` | `createBook` | A created `Book` with a server-assigned ID |
| `GET /api/v1/books/{bookId}` | `getBook` | One `Book`, or a stable error body |
| `GET /api/v1/books?limit=20` | `listBooks` | At most 100 books |

`operationId` becomes the generated Java method name. Component schemas remove repetition and
give successful and error responses one shared vocabulary.

The request model requires a non-blank title of at most 200 characters. Publication year is
optional and constrained to 1450–2100. ISBN is optional and accepts an ISBN-10 or ISBN-13 written
without separators. The response model alone owns the read-only ID: clients do not assign it.

The list operation has a default limit of 20 and a maximum of 100, and `BookList.books` also has
`maxItems: 100`. Applying the bound at the contract boundary makes the operational expectation
visible even before persistence exists. Tutorial 6 will add pagination and filtering.

`ErrorResponse` establishes `status`, machine-readable `code`, and human-readable `message` now.
Later tutorials can implement and extend error handling without changing its basic client shape.

## Beginner primer: OpenAPI structure

OpenAPI is a language-neutral description of an HTTP API. `paths` describe routes and methods;
`parameters` describe values in paths or queries; `requestBody` describes submitted JSON;
`responses` describe status codes and returned JSON; and reusable `schemas` describe object shape.

`required` says whether a value must exist. Constraints such as `minLength`, `maximum`, and
`pattern` say which values are accepted. Examples are documentation, not test data or defaults.
The generator translates this contract into Spring annotations, method signatures, Java types,
and Bean Validation annotations.

## Remove the placeholder feature

The template's Item controller, service, mapper, entity, repository, exceptions, tests, and Item
database migrations demonstrate the original placeholder contract. Remove those Item-specific
files after replacing that contract. Keeping them would leave maintained code coupled to generated
types that no longer exist. Retain the application, configuration, and actuator test.

This is useful compilation feedback: if you generate Books code and immediately run
`./gradlew compileJava`, references to `ItemsApi`, `Item`, and `ItemRequestBody` identify every
placeholder implementation that the contract change made stale. Remove the obsolete placeholder
slice, regenerate, and compile again. Tutorial 3 will add the Books implementation deliberately.

## Generate and inspect Java

Run generation explicitly:

```bash
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api:openApiGenerate
```

Inspect the local output under
`laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api/generated/src/main/java`. In particular, compare the
contract with `api/BooksApi.java` and `model/CreateBookRequest.java`.

Do not edit these files. `compileJava` depends on `openApiGenerate`, and `clean` deletes the entire
generated directory. The OpenAPI document and generator configuration are maintained source; the
Java output is disposable build output. Maintain the generator configuration in `build.gradle`,
but do not commit the generated-source directory.

The API module owns the contract and generated types. The service module depends on that module
and will implement `BooksApi` in Tutorial 3. This keeps HTTP types separate from runtime and later
persistence code.

## Verify the result

```bash
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api:clean \
  :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api:compileJava
./gradlew clean build
```

Confirm generation produces `BooksApi` and the four model classes, both commands end with
`BUILD SUCCESSFUL`, and `git status --short` does not show generated files.

## Ruby and Python orientation

This comparison is for orientation, not exact equivalence. The OpenAPI document plays some of the
contract role that routes, request serializers, and response schemas may share in Rails, FastAPI,
or Django REST Framework. Here it is the source used to generate the Java boundary; annotations in
a handwritten controller are not the primary contract.

## Challenge: understand the ISBN constraint

Using the contract above:

1. Add an optional ISBN field to both request and response schemas.
2. Accept exactly 10 characters ending in a digit or uppercase `X`, or exactly 13 digits, with no
   spaces or hyphens.
3. Add an ISBN example, regenerate, and locate the resulting validation annotation in
   `CreateBookRequest.java`.
4. Temporarily weaken the pattern and regenerate to observe that generated Java follows the
   contract, then restore the agreed constraint and run the clean build.


